WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.504
The payments app has grown.

2
00:00:04.504 --> 00:00:11.119
Three terabytes of payments, and on the busiest shopping day of the year, 25,000 new ones a second.

3
00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:16.789
One group of servers can't keep up any more, so we'll split the payments over several groups.

4
00:00:16.789 --> 00:00:18.736
But then a new question shows up.

5
00:00:18.736 --> 00:00:22.749
When the app wants one payment, how does it know which group has it?

6
00:00:22.749 --> 00:00:23.871
Hold that question.

7
00:00:24.133 --> 00:00:25.727
Start with what we have.

8
00:00:25.727 --> 00:00:33.164
MongoDB, a free database program, keeps its copies in a replica set: a group of servers that hold the same data.

9
00:00:33.164 --> 00:00:37.679
One of them, the primary, takes every write, and the others copy it.

10
00:00:37.867 --> 00:00:46.876
In the lesson's cloakroom, one attendant writes every coat in a notebook, and two assistants copy each line, so either can take over if he faints.

11
00:00:47.067 --> 00:00:48.801
That gives two limits.

12
00:00:48.801 --> 00:00:52.111
First, everything must fit on one machine.

13
00:00:52.111 --> 00:01:02.041
A 16 GB server gives its cache about 7.5 GB, so with 3 TB of payments, most reads go to disk, which is far slower than memory.

14
00:01:02.167 --> 00:01:05.759
Second, every write goes through one primary.

15
00:01:05.759 --> 00:01:10.628
Say it takes 8,000 payments a second at 70% of its processor.

16
00:01:10.628 --> 00:01:12.704
The busy day needs 25,000.

17
00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:18.332
More secondaries don't help, because each one has to replay all 25,000 writes too.

18
00:01:18.332 --> 00:01:24.028
Hiring more assistants to copy the notebook never made the attendant write any faster.

19
00:01:24.267 --> 00:01:27.482
So we split the payments over several replica sets.

20
00:01:27.482 --> 00:01:35.992
That's called sharding, and each replica set that holds a part is a shard: one attendant, with his own assistants copying his notebook.

21
00:01:36.233 --> 00:01:40.166
The obvious first version puts the rule in the app.

22
00:01:40.166 --> 00:01:45.100
Accounts A-0000 to A-4999 go to shard 1, the rest go to shard 2.

23
00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:47.561
That works, until shard 1 fills up.

24
00:01:47.561 --> 00:01:56.023
Adding a third shard means changing every copy of the program at the same moment, and moving payments while new ones keep arriving.

25
00:01:56.200 --> 00:02:00.111
It's like reprinting every ticket in the theatre while the show is on.

26
00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:01.886
Here's the better idea.

27
00:02:01.886 --> 00:02:08.669
Take the rule out of the code and make it data: a table of key ranges, and the shard that holds each one.

28
00:02:08.867 --> 00:02:12.147
In the cloakroom, that's a board by the door.

29
00:02:12.147 --> 00:02:14.333
Tickets 1 to 300, counter one.

30
00:02:14.333 --> 00:02:16.083
301 to 600, counter two.

31
00:02:16.083 --> 00:02:22.205
And a porter stands next to it, reads the board, and sends you to the right counter.

32
00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:23.274
Let's map it.

33
00:02:23.274 --> 00:02:27.441
The board is a table that MongoDB keeps on the config servers.

34
00:02:27.441 --> 00:02:33.087
It's now the most important data in the system, so it gets a replica set of its own.

35
00:02:33.233 --> 00:02:35.629
The porter is a router called mongos.

36
00:02:35.629 --> 00:02:39.903
It keeps a copy of the board in memory and stores nothing on disk.

37
00:02:40.067 --> 00:02:46.111
The app talks only to mongos, so you run several, often one next to each copy of the app.

38
00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:48.438
Which field does the board go by?

39
00:02:48.438 --> 00:02:51.218
You choose it, and it's called the shard key.

40
00:02:51.218 --> 00:02:57.087
All its possible values are cut into ranges called chunks, and each chunk belongs to one shard.

41
00:02:57.300 --> 00:03:04.511
The first chunk starts at MinKey, a value below every other, and the last ends at MaxKey, above every other.

42
00:03:04.511 --> 00:03:07.383
So every possible key has exactly one home.

43
00:03:07.567 --> 00:03:11.756
In the lesson's lab, the key is a hash of the payment's id.

44
00:03:11.756 --> 00:03:18.856
MongoDB turns each id into a big, random-looking number, and the chunks own ranges of those numbers.

45
00:03:18.856 --> 00:03:20.773
Below zero goes to shard 2.

46
00:03:20.773 --> 00:03:22.761
Zero and up goes to shard 1.

47
00:03:22.933 --> 00:03:30.535
For an insert, mongos reads the key, finds the chunk that holds it, and sends the insert to that shard's primary.

48
00:03:30.833 --> 00:03:32.981
Now we insert 20,000 payments.

49
00:03:32.981 --> 00:03:35.916
How do they split between the two shards?

50
00:03:35.916 --> 00:03:37.061
Pause and guess.

51
00:03:40.300 --> 00:03:44.584
10,079 on shard 1 and 9,921 on shard 2.

52
00:03:44.584 --> 00:03:49.636
Almost half and half, and no data had to move.

53
00:03:49.900 --> 00:03:52.511
Next, look up one payment by its id.

54
00:03:52.511 --> 00:03:59.620
The filter gives the shard key, so mongos hashes the id, reads the board, and asks only one shard.

55
00:04:00.300 --> 00:04:04.293
explain, which reports how a query ran, says single shard.

56
00:04:04.293 --> 00:04:05.945
That's a targeted query.

57
00:04:06.200 --> 00:04:09.014
Now ask for one shop's failed payments.

58
00:04:09.014 --> 00:04:11.107
That filter has no shard key.

59
00:04:11.107 --> 00:04:13.200
Which shards does mongos ask?

60
00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:14.355
Pause and guess.

61
00:04:17.567 --> 00:04:18.285
Every one.

62
00:04:18.285 --> 00:04:22.382
Each shard runs the query, and mongos merges the answers.

63
00:04:22.382 --> 00:04:24.179
explain says shard merge.

64
00:04:24.179 --> 00:04:26.767
That's called a scatter-gather read.

65
00:04:27.067 --> 00:04:28.831
Why can't mongos do better?

66
00:04:28.831 --> 00:04:35.168
Each shard indexes only its own payments, so nothing tells mongos where that shop's payments sit.

67
00:04:35.168 --> 00:04:40.591
A scatter-gather read is as slow as the slowest shard, and every shard pays for it.

68
00:04:40.767 --> 00:04:44.889
It's the porter shouting at every counter, has anyone seen a red scarf?

69
00:04:44.889 --> 00:04:47.735
Every attendant stops what they're doing to look.

70
00:04:47.933 --> 00:04:53.654
And one more limit: if the config servers can't pick a primary, the board can't change.

71
00:04:53.654 --> 00:04:56.416
Reads and writes go on, but no data moves.

72
00:04:56.700 --> 00:05:05.581
So the moral of the story: shards hold the payments, the config servers hold the board, and mongos reads the board for every request.

73
00:05:05.800 --> 00:05:08.983
Give it the shard key, and it goes to one counter.

74
00:05:08.983 --> 00:05:11.211
Leave it out, and it asks them all.

75
00:05:11.500 --> 00:05:12.658
One quick check.

76
00:05:12.658 --> 00:05:16.420
Support looks up a payment by its id and its status.

77
00:05:16.420 --> 00:05:18.518
Which shards does mongos ask?

78
00:05:18.518 --> 00:05:19.676
Pause and think.

79
00:05:22.900 --> 00:05:23.540
Just one.

80
00:05:23.540 --> 00:05:28.451
The id is the shard key, so mongos can hash it and go straight there.

81
00:05:28.451 --> 00:05:30.941
The status only narrows the answer.

82
00:05:31.267 --> 00:05:33.017
Everything hangs on that key.

83
00:05:33.017 --> 00:05:37.424
Next, we'll see what happens when the key is the time a payment was made.
